**Summary of the ADR:**

**Recommendation: Redis Streams.** The decision is driven by the constraints in the system context:

| Factor | Redis Streams | Apache Kafka |
|---|---|---|
| Team readiness | Already operate Redis | No Kafka experience |
| Setup time | Days (within 2-week constraint) | 3–4 weeks minimum |
| Throughput at our scale | Sufficient (~100K+ msg/s vs. our ~5K req/s peak) | Overkill for current + 10× growth |
| Operational complexity | Low — existing Redis instance | High — new cluster, new failure domain |
| Cost | Incremental memory on existing Redis | $500–1,500/mo managed, or self-hosted ops burden |
| Exactly-once | Application-level idempotency (required even with Kafka) | Native transactional EOS (but still needs consumer idempotency for safety) |
| Message retention | Memory-bounded (requires monitoring) | Disk-based (more forgiving) |

The key insight: even Kafka's exactly-once semantics require application-level idempotency to be truly safe, so we must build that logic regardless. Given that, Redis Streams' at-least-once delivery with `XACK`/`XPENDING` plus application-level deduplication gives us the same effective guarantee with a fraction of the operational cost.
